  "Question": "The value of \\(x^2-6x+13\\) can never be less than:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(4\\)",
    "B": "\\(4.5\\)",
    "C": "\\(5\\)",
    "D": "\\(7\\)",
    "E": "\\(13\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "To find a lower bound, rewrite the expression as a square plus a constant. The first two terms match \\((x-3)^2=x^2-6x+9\\), so\n\\[\nx^2-6x+13=(x-3)^2+4.\n\\]\nFor every real \\(x\\), the square \\((x-3)^2\\) is nonnegative. Hence the expression is at least \\(4\\), and it equals \\(4\\) when \\(x=3\\). Its minimum is therefore \\(4\\), choice A.",
